.TH IDLEPILOT 1 "July 2026" "idlepilot 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
idlepilot \- fail-closed, condition-gated process supervision for Linux
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B idlepilot
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B idlepilot
starts one reviewed executable without a shell only while every configured
local condition is proven true.  The built-in Linux probe can require a local
time window, a systemd-logind idle hint, Wi-Fi carrier, and external power.
An unknown observation fails closed.
.PP
While waiting, conditions are polled at the configured late-poll interval.
While an action is running, all conditions are rechecked at the configured
guard interval.  Loss of a guard sends SIGTERM immediately followed by
SIGKILL to the action process group and terminates the supervisor with exit 7.
.PP
The program is Linux-only, refuses root and setuid execution, and has no GUI,
network listener, or arbitrary-exec control endpoint.  Output is JSON or JSON
Lines by default.  Add
.B --human
for terse text intended for people.
.PP
Ordinary command errors are also emitted as JSON, or as human text with
.BR --human .
A terminal supervisor error that cannot prove its process group or background
state writer quiescent emits neither form: it returns only a nonzero exit code
so a full pipe or journal cannot delay process exit and cgroup cleanup.
.PP
Single-result commands and doctor snapshots treat stdout EPIPE as a consumer
that has closed normally: they do not panic or replace the command's own exit
decision.  The run JSONL event sink instead treats a write failure as an
operating-system supervision error.  Consumers must drain run output through
EOF rather than piping it to an early-exit program such as head.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init --config PATH --executable PATH --state-file PATH [OPTIONS]
Create a mode 0600 schema-v1 canonical configuration.  Optional arguments are
.B --name ID
and
.B --working-directory
.IR PATH ;
repeatable
.B --arg VALUE
and
.BR --env KEY=VALUE ;
policies
.BR --window ,
.BR --wifi ,
.BR --power ,
and
.BR --idle ;
timing
.BR --poll-seconds ,
.BR --guard-milliseconds ,
.BR --start-stability-seconds ,
.BR --idle-seconds ,
and
.BR --stop-grace-seconds ;
runtime
.B --max-runtime-seconds
or
.BR --no-runtime-limit ;
and retry controls
.BR --max-attempts-per-window ,
.BR --retry-on-failure ,
and
.BR --no-retry-after-guard-loss .
Repeated argv order is retained and duplicate environment keys are rejected.
The executable SHA-256 is calculated and pinned.  Existing configurations are
not overwritten.  Missing configuration and state parent paths are created
with mode 0700.  An existing final parent must be a non-symlink directory
owned by the current user with mode 0700.
.TP
.B validate --config PATH
Parse the configuration, enforce file and runtime-path security invariants,
and report warnings for weakened conditions or a missing digest pin.
Digest syntax is checked here; content equality is checked at launch or with
the explicit digest command.
.TP
.B check --config PATH
Take one complete condition snapshot.  Exit zero only when all conditions are
met; exit 3 for false or unknown conditions.
.TP
.B plan --config PATH
Read configuration, conditions, executable digest, durable state, and recorded
process identities without taking the instance lock, reserving an attempt, or
creating state.  Stable decisions are ready, conditions_blocked,
artifact_mismatch, daemon_running, recovery_required, already_completed,
attempts_exhausted, and invalid_snapshot.  Ready exits 0; ordinary no-launch
decisions exit 3; artifact_mismatch, recovery_required, and invalid_snapshot
require operator attention and exit 5.  A ready plan is only a snapshot; run
repeats the lock, digest, and final-guard checks.
.PP
Recovery_required includes a persisted fault or, when no owning daemon is
alive, any persisted action identity.  It remains recovery_required when the
recorded leader and process group are both gone: process absence cannot prove
the action's terminal result or absence of external side effects.
.TP
.B doctor --config PATH [--watch-seconds N]
Emit snapshots about once per second for 1 to 3600 seconds (default 30).
Exit zero if eligibility was observed at least once, otherwise exit 3.  No
action is started.
.TP
.B run --config PATH [--once]
Run the supervisor and emit JSONL audit events.  Daemon mode waits and polls;
.B --once
performs one eligibility decision and reserves at most one durable launch
intent.  If the final guard remains true and no termination signal arrives,
the action is launched and monitored synchronously until it exits or is
stopped.  A final guard change, signal, or crash after intent persistence can
consume the attempt without an exec.
.TP
.B status --config PATH
Read durable state and verify the daemon PID with its /proc start ticks.
The JSON includes daemon and action liveness, an action_status classification,
attention_required, attempt/window counters, PID/PGID observations, last exit
code or signal, and the state update timestamp.  Valid stopped or attention
states still exit 0; consumers must inspect the fields.
.PP
Stable action_status values are none, recorded_process_running,
terminal_result_unknown, descendant_group_running, pid_reused,
identity_incomplete, and launch_intent_unresolved.  Terminal_result_unknown
means that the recorded leader and group are gone but no durable terminal
result exists; it has action_alive false and attention_required true.  A
persisted fault also requires attention even if action_status is none.
.TP
.B stop --config PATH [--wait-seconds N]
Open a pidfd, verify its /proc start ticks, send SIGTERM through that exact
kernel process handle, and poll the pidfd for 1 to 60 seconds (default 15).
A missing daemon is a successful no-op.  If pidfd support is unavailable,
stop fails closed instead of falling back to a race-prone numeric PID signal.
.TP
.B digest --file PATH
Validate a regular input file no larger than 512 MiB and print its SHA-256.
.TP
.B import --source PATH --artifact-dir PATH
Copy a reviewed input into a private content-addressed directory under its
SHA-256 name with mode 0500.  Import is idempotent for identical content.
.TP
.B schema
Print the stable machine-interface capabilities, plan decisions, API version,
configuration schema version 1, and durable-state schema version 2.
.TP
.B version
Print the program and API versions.
.TP
.B help
Print command help.
.PP
Validation, check, plan, doctor, status, and digest do not create a missing
directory or state file.  For the configuration-based commands, the configured
state parent must already be a caller-owned mode 0700 directory.  Init creates
configuration/state parents.  An explicit run may create only the configured
private state-parent chain before mutating state and its lock.  Stop does not
create files but can signal a verified daemon.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The UTF-8 format is one
.I key = value
per line.  Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored.  Values may be
unquoted or double-quoted; the quoted escapes are backslash, quote, newline,
carriage return, and tab.  There is no shell, environment-variable, tilde,
glob, or inline-comment expansion.
.PP
All keys below are required except
.B executable_sha256
and
.BR max_runtime_seconds .
Only
.B arg
and
.B env
may repeat.
.TP
.B schema_version = 1
Configuration schema.
.TP
.B name = ID
Identifier matching [A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,63}.
.TP
.B executable = PATH
Absolute path to the regular executable or reviewed shebang script.
.TP
.B arg = STRING
One literal argv entry, in order.  It is never shell-parsed.
.TP
.B working_directory = PATH
Absolute action working directory.
.TP
.B env = KEY=value
One explicit non-secret environment entry.  Inherited environment is cleared.
Dangerous loader and interpreter keys are rejected.
.TP
.B executable_sha256 = HEX
Optional 64-character lowercase SHA-256 content pin.
.TP
.B window = HH:MM-HH:MM
Half-open local-time interval.  It may cross midnight; equal endpoints are
rejected.
.TP
.B poll_seconds = N
Waiting-state poll interval, 10 through 600.  No configuration can delay the
late poll beyond ten minutes.
.TP
.B guard_milliseconds = N
Qualifying/running guard interval, 100 through 250.  The generated default is
250 ms; the bounded loginctl query also has a 250 ms timeout.  The normal Linux
adapter targets action-stop initiation within one second, but this is not a
hard real-time guarantee.
.TP
.B start_stability_seconds = N
Required continuously eligible time before launch, 0 through 300.
.TP
.B idle_seconds = N
Minimum logind-reported idle duration, 0 through 86400.
.TP
.B wifi = any|interface:NAME|disabled
Wi-Fi carrier policy.
.TP
.B power = auto|required|ignore
External-power policy.  Auto requires external power when a system battery or
portable DMI chassis is discovered.  Without a battery, only an explicitly
stationary DMI chassis passes without external power.
.TP
.B idle = logind-seat:NAME|logind-user|disabled
Idle provider.
.TP
.B stop_grace_seconds = N
Seconds between process-group TERM and KILL, 1 through 300.
.TP
.B max_runtime_seconds = N
Optional action runtime cap, 1 through 604800.
.TP
.B max_attempts_per_window = N
Durable launch-intent limit per local window, 1 through 100.  The intent is
persisted before spawn, so a final guard change, termination signal, or crash
can consume an attempt even when exec does not occur.
.TP
.B retry_on_failure = true|false
Whether a failed action may be attempted again in the same window.
.TP
.B retry_after_guard_loss = true|false
Whether a later supervisor instance may retry after conditions become true
again in the same window.  An operator SIGINT/SIGTERM shutdown is not a guard
loss and is not terminally completed solely because this value is false; its
durably reserved launch attempt still counts on the next evaluation.
.TP
.B state_file = PATH
Absolute durable state path in a caller-owned mode 0700 directory.
.
.SH FILE SECURITY
Security-sensitive paths must be absolute and must not contain dot, dot-dot,
symlink, or group- or world-writable existing directory components.  Consequently,
/tmp and /var/tmp are unsuitable.
.PP
Configuration and executable files must be regular non-symlink files owned by
the caller or root and not writable by group or others.  The executable must
have an execute bit.  The working directory must not be a symlink or group- or
world-writable.  State directories are caller-owned mode 0700; state and lock
files are mode 0600.
.
.SH DURABLE STATE
Durable state uses schema version 2 and contains every state key in canonical
form.  The config_fingerprint preimage is the UTF-8 canonical configuration
output with every line beginning with `#` removed and each retained line
terminated by one newline byte.  It therefore binds argv order, environment,
artifact pin, policies, and state path while ignoring generated comments.  A
pristine never-initialized state may be unbound; the supervisor binds it at its
first durable write.
.PP
Run, plan, status, and stop reject a fingerprint mismatch before launching or
signalling.  Changing configuration semantics therefore requires stopping the
service, verifying that the cgroup and any persisted action are gone,
preserving and rotating the old state, and starting a new state.  Rotation can
reset completion and attempt history, so duplicate side effects must be
reviewed.  Schema-v1 state is not migrated automatically and requires the same
procedure.
.PP
The same evidence-preserving recovery applies to an unresolved launch intent,
a persisted action without a durable terminal result, and a persisted fault.
.
.SH PROCESS CONTRACT
The action receives null standard input, output, and error, a cleared
environment, PATH=/usr/bin:/bin, LANG=C.UTF-8, and explicitly configured
environment entries.  It runs with no_new_privs, core dumps disabled, umask
0077, and a dedicated POSIX process group.
.PP
The action and its descendants must remain in that process group.  Programs
that call setsid, create a new process group, double-fork, or delegate work to
an external daemon can escape live guard cleanup and are unsupported.  A
systemd user service with KillMode=mixed provides cgroup cleanup when the unit
stops, but this is not a hostile-code sandbox.
.PP
After child spawn, executable identity, the dedicated PGID, and process start
ticks are rechecked.  On failure, cleanup has at most two seconds to prove both
leader reap and emptiness of a process group proven to be owned by that child.
If either fact cannot be proved, the error requires process exit and follows
the terminal no-output path described above.
.PP
Before reserving a launch attempt, idlepilot preflights pidfd support on its
own process.  It opens a child pidfd immediately after spawn and observes exit
without reaping.  The unreaped leader reserves its numeric PGID while
/proc process-group field 5 is scanned and descendants are cleaned; the leader
is reaped only after two empty scans five milliseconds apart show that no
other group member remains.  Only an exit observed
before the first stop signal overrides a guard, runtime, or shutdown reason.
A code-zero status first observed after signalling can be a TERM handler and
keeps the original stop policy.
.PP
After spawn, running state is persisted in the background while action
monitoring continues.  Its finite deadline is the greater of fifteen seconds and
the configured guard interval.  Fast actions that already exited after the
post-spawn observation skip this unnecessary running-state write.  The bound
allows transient home-storage writeback stalls because launch intent is
already durable.  If that deadline
expires, or if an empty
process group cannot be confirmed after cleanup, idlepilot skips later audit
and final-state I/O and retains the instance lock until process exit.  An
embedding caller sees an Error whose requires_process_exit method returns true
and must terminate immediately without blocking log, flush, or cleanup.  The
packaged systemd service supplies cgroup cleanup as the backstop.
.PP
OS thread creation for the background writer is fallible.  A creation failure
is returned as an error only after the live action group has been stopped and
proved empty; uncertain cleanup instead requires immediate process exit.
.PP
If an earlier daemon dies after persisting a launch intent but before durable
action identity or normal final state, the next startup recognizes state with
phase qualifying, reason launch_intent_persisted, daemon identity, and no
action identity as unresolved.  It fails closed instead of risking a duplicate
exec.  Stop and verify the old cgroup and processes, preserve and rotate the
state file, and only then restart.  This startup security error is an ordinary
reported error, not a requires-process-exit error in the new process.
.PP
Any persisted action identity is also rejected when its owning daemon is gone.
If its leader and group are gone, status calls the state
terminal_result_unknown: cleanup may be complete, but success, failure,
external side effects, and terminal-state persistence remain unknown.  A
persisted fault is likewise rejected regardless of whether action identity is
present.  Plan reports recovery_required and supervisor startup fails with a
security error until the evidence is reviewed and the state is preserved and
rotated.
.
.SH BUILD COMPATIBILITY
The supported family is 64-bit GNU/Linux with /proc and /sys.  Continuous
integration executes tests and release packaging on x86-64 Ubuntu GNU/Linux.
The aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu job is a compile check only; it does not link,
run, or package the product.  musl targets are not currently validated.
.PP
Action supervision and the stop command require Linux pidfd_open,
pidfd_send_signal, and pidfd polling (Linux 5.3 or newer) on x86-64 or
aarch64.  Capability is checked before an action launch, and there is no
numeric-PID fallback.
.PP
A native release archive contains an ELF linked against the build host's glibc
and other system libraries.  It does not promise compatibility with an older
glibc distribution.  Build on the oldest supported deployment environment or
produce and smoke-test distribution-specific packages.
.
.SH SYSTEMD
The packaged template expects
.I %h/.config/idlepilot/%i.conf
and runs as a user service.  Start an instance with:
.PP
.nf
systemctl --user enable --now idlepilot@NAME.service
.fi
.PP
The template has a strict read-only filesystem view and grants write access to
private temporary storage and the conventional idlepilot state, share, and
user-runtime directories.  Add only required output paths with an instance drop-in
.BR ReadWritePaths= .
The service restarts after guard-loss and runtime-limit exits so a fresh
supervisor can resume fail-closed waiting after systemd cgroup cleanup.
Runtime-limit state is terminal for the current local window; guard-loss state
is retryable only when configured accordingly.  An unresolved launch intent
requires operator cleanup verification and state preservation/rotation.  So do
terminal_result_unknown and persisted fault states; do not blindly restart
them.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful command, completed action, or already-completed window.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error.
.TP
.B 3
Not eligible, no eligible doctor sample, attempts exhausted, or an ordinary
plan no-launch decision.
.TP
.B 4
Probe error.
.TP
.B 5
Security invariant failure, including an unresolved launch-intent startup
fence, unknown durable terminal result, persisted fault,
configuration/state fingerprint mismatch, or a plan attention decision.
.TP
.B 6
Process error or failed action.
.TP
.B 7
Live guard lost and action stopped.
.TP
.B 8
Runtime cap stopped the action.
.TP
.B 70
State, operating-system, or internal error.
.TP
.B 130, 143
Supervisor terminated by SIGINT or SIGTERM respectively.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The CLI uses the process environment only as normal operating-system context.
The supervised action does not inherit it.  Configuration paths never expand
HOME or other environment variables.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I %h/.config/idlepilot/NAME.conf
Conventional per-instance configuration used by the packaged user unit.
.TP
.I %h/.local/state/idlepilot/NAME.state
Recommended durable state location.
.TP
.I %h/.local/lib/idlepilot/artifacts/SHA256
Recommended content-addressed action location.
.TP
.I %h/.local/share/idlepilot/
Default packaged-unit writable workspace root.
.
